# The image build context only needs server/ + web/ + apk/. Keep everything else out so
# the (slow, multi-arch) build stays lean and a docs/compose change can't invalidate it.
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/data
.git
.gitignore
.github
docs
screenshots
android
**/*.md
# …except the release notes, which SHIP in the image: the admin panel's "What's new" reads
# /app/CHANGELOG.md so the notes always describe the version running. It is copied in the
# last runtime step, so editing it only invalidates that one cheap layer.
!CHANGELOG.md

# ── Secrets: never let one enter the build context ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Docker does NOT read .gitignore. These patterns are all in .gitignore, so nothing here can ever
# be COMMITTED — but an UNTRACKED file sitting in the working tree still enters the context, and
# `COPY server/ ./` would carry it into the image. That matters more than it looks:
#
#   COPY apk/ ./public/download/
#
# puts everything in apk/ into the PUBLIC web root, and /download is one of the few paths reachable
# over the Cloudflare tunnel. A keystore left in apk/ after a local signing experiment would be
# served to the internet. Cheap to prevent, and impossible to notice if it ever happened.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.jks
**/*.keystore
**/*.pem
**/*.p12
**/*.key
# NOT `apk/*.apk`. That is the one artifact that MUST enter the context: CI downloads the freshly
# built APK into apk/ and `COPY apk/ ./public/download/` is what bundles it. Excluding it would
# publish an image with no app to install, and the "Confirm APK is present" step would not catch it
# — that checks the filesystem, which is exactly where the file would still be.
